mourners queuing in their hundreds to  pay respects to one of the government  soldiers killed in an attack by forces  loyal to warlord Khalifa Haftar he loved  men died and nearly a hundred were  wounded on Sunday as they defended the  small town of Abu Ghraib 80 kilometers  east of Misrata  they included Mohammed Abu zaid's 23  year old son Abdul Aziz who volunteered  to help repel the half tower attack I  have another 12 sons and I would  sacrifice them all in this struggle  against a man who wants to be a dictator  these images were put on social media  it's thought that at least 12 of  half-dollars men were killed in the  fighting the attack took everyone by  surprise we know there was a ceasefire  and we weren't expecting an attack there  was a lot of firepower  he suffered shrapnel wounds to the head  I chest and hand the museum on Tripoli  Street tells the story of Misrata spree  vyas battles in the 2011 revolution and  more recently against ISIL in Libya the  fighters of Misrata are known for their  bravery it's what saved the city nine  years ago and was a key factor in the  downfall of Muammar Gaddafi but the  people here also know what the cost can  be when you oppose a dictator  in the 2011 revolution Gaddafi's forces  occupied the city for three months 2,800  people were killed and more than 13,000  were wounded but it was the men from is  rata who captured the Libyan leader  insert and brought the war against him  to a close here they believe the warlord  Khalifa Haftar is just like Gaddafi he  wants to rule Libya the same way as  Gaddafi but we fought against a  dictatorship which controlled us for 40  years and we won't allow that to happen  again  few here thought the sacrifices of the  Revolution would have to be made again  and few here are willing to predict when  the day will come when Misrata no longer  has to mourn the deaths of its young men  Tony Berkley al-jazeera Misrata
 

